          "id": "spring-boot-auto-configuration",
          "heading": "Spring Boot Auto-Configuration",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Auto-configuration is a feature of Spring Boot that automatically configures your Spring application based on the dependencies present on the classpath.",
                "Example: "
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 22,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "external-configuration",
          "heading": "External Configuration",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "External configuration allows developers to customize or override the default settings provided by Spring Boot through properties files, YAML files, environment variables, or command-line arguments.",
                "Example: "
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 26,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "why-external-configuration-matters",
          "heading": "Why External Configuration Matters",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Spring Boot provides sensible defaults (convention over configuration), but you may need to customize behavior in real-world applications, such as:"
            },
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Connecting to a production database.",
                "Modifying logging levels.",
                "Defining application-specific settings."
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 31,
          "subsections": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "how-to-customize-spring-boot-configuration",
      "heading": "How to Customize Spring Boot Configuration",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [],
      "wordCount": 0,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "id": "applicationproperties-file",
          "heading": "application.properties file",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The most common way to configure Spring Boot applications."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Example:"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "",
              "code": "# MySQL database connection\nspring.datasource.url=jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/db_irs\nspring.datasource.username=root\nspring.datasource.password=root\n\n# Set logging level\nlogging.level.root=INFO",
              "runnable": false
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Located typically at src/main/resources/application.properties."
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 14,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "applicationyml-file",
          "heading": "application.yml file",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "YAML is an alternative to .properties and allows hierarchical configuration."
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 10,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "environment-variables",
          "heading": "Environment Variables",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Useful for containerized environments or cloud deployments."
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 7,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "command-line-arguments",
          "heading": "Command-Line Arguments",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Passed at runtime and useful for overriding configuration quickly during testing or deployment."
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 13,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "default-values",
          "heading": "Default Values",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Spring Boot defines default values for all known configuration properties. If no external configuration is provided, these defaults are used."
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 20,
          "subsections": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "location-priority-of-configuration-files",
      "heading": "Location Priority of Configuration Files",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Spring Boot looks for configuration files in the following locations, in descending order of priority:"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": true,
          "items": [
            "/config subdirectory of the current directory",
            "Current directory",
            "classpath:/config/ (e.g., src/main/resources/config)",
            "classpath:/ (e.g., src/main/resources)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Priority Rule:"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "If the same property is defined in multiple places, the one in the higher priority location overrides the others."
        }
      ],
      "wordCount": 50,
      "subsections": []
    },
    {
      "id": "example-scenario-logging-level-conflict",
      "heading": "Example Scenario: Logging Level Conflict",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Suppose you have two application.properties files:"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "src/main/resources/application.properties: logging.level.root=INFO",
            "src/main/resources/config/application.properties: logging.level.root=WARN"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Result:"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The logger level will be WARN because classpath:/config/ has higher priority than the root."
        }
      ],
      "wordCount": 25,
      "subsections": []
    },
    {
      "id": "injecting-configuration-in-spring-bean",
      "heading": "Injecting Configuration in Spring Bean",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Example Code:"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "language": "java",
          "code": "package com.infosys.irs.model;\n\nimport org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;\nimport org.springframework.stereotype.Component;\n\n@Component\npublic class TestBean {\n    @Value(\"${logging.level.root}\")\n    private String rootLogLevel;\n\n    // Additional logic\n}",
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "This demonstrates how to access configuration values directly in your Java code using Spring’s @Value annotation."
        }
      ],
      "wordCount": 18,
      "subsections": []
    },
